 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision and retains the revision history. Browsers display a revisioned view of the board.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets. The delivery component holds each browser's connection and streams committed revisions as they happen; the change store exposes revisions in commit order so a reconnecting browser can replay what it missed; the browser owns subscription, reconnect, and catch-up. This route delivers updates with low latency without coupling the board to a hosted realtime provider. In exchange, the project owns connection capacity, reliable publication, and reconnect handling itself.
 
